(ned ROTARY FACING -- stock prep. Skim a bar down to a target diameter.)
(Operator 2026-08-12: tool number, starting D, end D, a length, and ONE)
(cutting number -- chip load. Everything else is solved from it.)

(START AT G55 X0 Y0. Within 0.1 mm the cycle moves there itself; further out)
(it refuses, because G54 is 3.67 m away and the air offset is 1000 mm, so a)
(big number is never a near miss. Y0 is the start and LENGTH runs +Y.)

(DIAMETER IS Z. G55 Z0 sits on the B centreline, so the tool tip at Z = D/2)
(IS diameter D. Every Z below is a RADIUS. X is never commanded.)

(CHIP LOAD IS THE ONLY CUTTING INPUT. The bar turning under the cutter IS)
(the feed, exactly like turning, and its speed is w * r -- linear in radius,)
(so the same B speed is a different chip load on a fat bar and a thin one.)
(Solved per pass at the UNCUT radius, the fastest-moving material in the)
(cut, so the real chip load never exceeds what was asked for.)

(  surface speed at r     v = wB * pi * r / 3        mm/min, wB in deg/s)
(  chip load              fz = v / [S * N]           N = flutes)
(  =>  S = wB * pi * r / [3 * fz * N])

(ONE SPINDLE SPEED FOR THE WHOLE CYCLE, B DOES THE VARYING. Operator)
(2026-08-12: "make B end at 8rpm on the final pass, so that it never goes)
(beyond that limit". Surface speed is w times r, so holding the chip load)
(with a fixed spindle means B must RISE as the stock thins. Size the spindle)
(off the SMALLEST uncut radius the cycle will ever see and B arrives at its)
(ceiling exactly on the last pass and is below it everywhere before that.)
(The spindle then never changes mid-cut, which is the sane way round: a)
(re-solved S every pass had the drive chasing a new speed while engaged.)
(If the answer lands above the drive ceiling the spindle caps and B simply)
(runs slower still. If it lands BELOW the drive floor the machine cannot cut)
(that heavy at all, so the cycle aborts and reports the largest chip load it)
(could actually deliver rather than quietly cutting something lighter.)

(THE TOOL COMPENSATION IS IN Y. The tool bottom is a disc: sweeping its)
(CENTRE from 0 to LENGTH machines -R to LENGTH+R. To finish exactly between)
(the two edges the centre runs R to LENGTH-R.)

(D START IS A PROMISE, NOT A MEASUREMENT. Nothing here can see the stock, so)
(an optimistic number buries the tool on the first pass.)
o<rotary_face> sub
  (ARG LINES ARE A CONTRACT WITH THE GUI: SubCallButton parses this exact)
  (shape and fills each one from the widget of the same name.)
  #<rf_tool>   = #1 (=0 tool number - required)
  #<rf_dstart> = #2 (=0 stock diameter now mm - required)
  #<rf_dend>   = #3 (=0 target diameter mm - required)
  #<rf_len>    = #4 (=0 length to face, +Y from zero - required)
  #<rf_chip>   = #5 (=0 chip load mm per tooth - required)
  #<rf_vc>     = #13 (=0 surface speed m per min - 0 keeps the old behaviour)
  #<rf_flutes> = #6 (=2 flutes - from the tool table)
  #<rf_fdepth> = #7 (=0 flute depth mm - from the tool table, 0 = unknown)
  #<rf_bmax>   = #8 (=120.0 B max deg/s - JOINT_6 MAX_VELOCITY, 20 rpm)
  #<rf_smin>   = #9 (=1000 spindle floor rpm - SPINDLE_0 MIN_FORWARD_VELOCITY)
  #<rf_smax>   = #10 (=18000 spindle ceiling rpm - SPINDLE_0 MAX_FORWARD_VELOCITY)
  #<rf_step>   = #11 (=0.5 Y advance per B turn as a fraction of tool diameter)
  (SPINDLE OVERRIDE. 0 means use the rpm solved from the chip load. Any)
  (other value runs the spindle THERE and leaves B, the Y feed and the)
  (depths exactly as solved -- so the chip load the tool actually sees is)
  (no longer the one that was asked for. That is the point of an override)
  (and it is the operator's call, but the page states the real chip load.)
  #<rf_srpm>   = #12 (=0 spindle override rpm - 0 uses the calculated one)
  #<pi> = 3.14159265
  o1 if [#<rf_flutes> LE 0]
    #<rf_flutes> = 2
  o1 endif
  o2 if [#<rf_bmax> LE 0]
    #<rf_bmax> = 120.0
  o2 endif
  o3 if [#<rf_smin> LE 0]
    #<rf_smin> = 1000
  o3 endif
  o4 if [#<rf_smax> LE 0]
    #<rf_smax> = 18000
  o4 endif
  o5 if [#<rf_step> LE 0]
    #<rf_step> = 0.5
  o5 endif
  o10 if [#<_metric> EQ 0]
    (abort, ROTARY FACE: switch to MM first)
  o10 endif
  o11 if [#<rf_tool> LE 0]
    (abort, ROTARY FACE: no tool number given)
  o11 endif
  o12 if [#<rf_dstart> LE 0 OR #<rf_dend> LE 0]
    (abort, ROTARY FACE: both start and end diameter are required)
  o12 endif
  o13 if [#<rf_dend> GE #<rf_dstart>]
    (abort, ROTARY FACE: end diameter must be SMALLER than the start diameter)
  o13 endif
  o14 if [#<rf_len> LE 0]
    (abort, ROTARY FACE: length must be positive - it runs +Y from zero)
  o14 endif
  o19 if [#<rf_chip> LE 0]
    (abort, ROTARY FACE: chip load is required - it is the only cutting number)
  o19 endif
  o25 if [#<rf_srpm> GT 0 AND #<rf_srpm> LT #<rf_smin>]
    (abort, ROTARY FACE: spindle override #<rf_srpm> is below the drive floor #<rf_smin>)
  o25 endif
  o26 if [#<rf_srpm> GT #<rf_smax>]
    (abort, ROTARY FACE: spindle override #<rf_srpm> is above the drive ceiling #<rf_smax>)
  o26 endif

  (IN THE RIGHT FRAME FIRST. X0 and Y0 in G54 are somewhere else entirely, so)
  (the position checks below mean nothing without this one. 1 is G54, 2 G55.)
  o15 if [#5220 NE 2]
    (abort, ROTARY FACE: not in G55 - the rotary zero is G55 X0 Y0 Z0, G54 is scratch)
  o15 endif
  o16 if [ABS[#5420] GT 0.1]
    (abort, ROTARY FACE: X is #5420 - jog to G55 X0 before starting)
  o16 endif
  o17 if [ABS[#5421] GT 0.1]
    (abort, ROTARY FACE: Y is #5421 - jog to G55 Y0 before starting)
  o17 endif

  (RADIUS FROM HERE DOWN. The operator types a DIAMETER; converted once here)
  (so that nothing below can mix the two.)
  #<rstart> = [#<rf_dstart> / 2.0]
  #<rend>   = [#<rf_dend> / 2.0]
  (Z MUST ALREADY CLEAR THE STOCK, because the next move is LATERAL. Below)
  (rstart the tool is inside the swept envelope and going to X0 Y0 drives it)
  (through the bar. Checked before the spindle starts, so a refusal is free.)
  o18 if [#5422 LE #<rstart>]
    (abort, ROTARY FACE: Z is #5422 and the stock radius is #<rstart> - raise Z clear first)
  o18 endif

  T#<rf_tool> M6
  G43
  o20 if [#5400 NE #<rf_tool>]
    (abort, ROTARY FACE: the spindle is not holding the tool that was asked for)
  o20 endif
  #<tdia> = #5410
  o21 if [#<tdia> LE 0]
    (abort, ROTARY FACE: that tool has no diameter in the table)
  o21 endif
  #<trad> = [#<tdia> / 2.0]

  (RADIAL DEPTH FROM THE TOOL, NOT FROM A GUESS. Operator 2026-08-12: half)
  (the tool diameter or 6.35 mm -- 1/4 in -- whichever is smaller, and never)
  (deeper than the flutes are long. A 12.7 mm tool takes 6.35 off the RADIUS,)
  (12.7 off the diameter, per pass.)
  #<doc> = #<trad>
  o24 if [#<rf_fdepth> GT 0 AND #<rf_fdepth> LT #<doc>]
    #<doc> = #<rf_fdepth>
  o24 endif

  #<ya> = #<trad>
  #<yb> = [#<rf_len> - #<trad>]
  o22 if [#<yb> LE #<ya>]
    (abort, ROTARY FACE: that length is shorter than the tool - nothing to sweep)
  o22 endif

  (THE HELIX ADVANCES HALF A TOOL DIAMETER PER TURN OF B, so each turn cuts)
  (into the wall the one before it left.)
  #<pitch> = [#<tdia> * #<rf_step>]

  (THE SMALLEST UNCUT RADIUS THE CYCLE WILL SEE. Passes step down by doc)
  (from rstart until rend, so the last pass starts at rstart minus doc times)
  (one less than the pass count. FIX truncates, hence the remainder test.)
  #<npass> = FIX[[#<rstart> - #<rend>] / #<doc>]
  o40 if [[#<rstart> - #<rend>] - [#<npass> * #<doc>] GT 0.0001]
    #<npass> = [#<npass> + 1]
  o40 endif
  o41 if [#<npass> LT 1]
    #<npass> = 1
  o41 endif
  #<routmin> = [#<rstart> - [[#<npass> - 1] * #<doc>]]
  (SURFACE SPEED SETS THE SPINDLE, CHIP LOAD SETS THE ROTATION.)
  (Operator 2026-08-12: "why is it a limit and not something that is)
  (functionally related". It was not related at all -- the tool diameter)
  (appeared nowhere in the spindle solve, so a 1.5 inch cutter would have)
  (been handed the same rpm as a 3/8 and run at 1077 m per min.)
  (  S  = Vc * 1000 / [pi * D]        the cutter and the material)
  (  wB = 3 * fz * S * N / [pi * r]   the chip load, at the uncut radius)
  (Change the tool and the rpm follows, because D is in the equation now.)
  (WITH NO Vc GIVEN the old behaviour is kept: solve the spindle from the)
  (chip load at the smallest uncut radius so B lands exactly on its ceiling)
  (on the last pass.)
  o45 if [#<rf_vc> GT 0]
    #<sp> = [#<rf_vc> * 1000.0 / [#<pi> * #<tdia>]]
  o45 else
    #<sp> = [#<rf_bmax> * #<pi> * #<routmin> / [3.0 * #<rf_chip> * #<rf_flutes>]]
  o45 endif
  o42 if [#<sp> GT #<rf_smax>]
    #<sp> = #<rf_smax>
  o42 endif
  o43 if [#<sp> LT #<rf_smin>]
    #<vcmin> = [#<rf_smin> * #<pi> * #<tdia> / 1000.0]
    (abort, ROTARY FACE: that needs #<sp> rpm and the spindle floor is #<rf_smin>. With this cutter the slowest surface speed the machine can hold is #<vcmin> m per min)
  o43 endif
  (SANITY: B must be able to deliver the chip load at this spindle speed.)
  #<wbneed> = [3.0 * #<rf_chip> * #<sp> * #<rf_flutes> / [#<pi> * #<routmin>]]
  o46 if [#<wbneed> GT #<rf_bmax>]
    #<fzmax> = [#<rf_bmax> * #<pi> * #<routmin> / [3.0 * #<sp> * #<rf_flutes>]]
    (abort, ROTARY FACE: B would need #<wbneed> deg per sec to hold that chip load at #<sp> rpm and its ceiling is #<rf_bmax>. Either drop the chip load to #<fzmax> mm per tooth or lower the surface speed)
  o46 endif
  #<sout> = #<sp>
  o44 if [#<rf_srpm> GT 0]
    #<sout> = #<rf_srpm>
  o44 endif
  #<len>   = [#<yb> - #<ya>]
  (NEVER PLUNGE. Operator 2026-08-12: "i am using a fly cutter that doesn't)
  (cut in the middle and as it goes it with a plunge, i get rubbing. ensure)
  (that you always take at least 3 cutter diameters to ramp half cutter)
  (diameter of plunge".)
  (A cutter with no centre cutting edge has nothing to remove material with)
  (at its axis, so any descent steeper than the flutes can shear turns into)
  (the dead centre rubbing the bottom of the cut.)
  (THE GRADIENT: 3 tool diameters of Y for half a tool diameter of Z. Since)
  (the radial depth IS half the diameter, that is 6 times the depth of Y)
  (travel per descent, which holds whatever the depth is capped to.)
  #<ramp> = [6.0 * #<doc>]
  o47 if [#<ramp> GE #<len>]
    (abort, ROTARY FACE: the cut is #<len> mm long and a no-plunge ramp for this cutter needs #<ramp> mm. Use a shorter tool, a shallower depth, or face a longer length)
  o47 endif
  #<revs>  = [#<len> / #<pitch>]
  (PRINT, ROTARY FACE: T#<rf_tool> dia #<tdia> with #<rf_flutes> flutes, chip load #<rf_chip> mm per tooth)
  (PRINT, ROTARY FACE: radial depth #<doc> mm per pass, Y advance #<pitch> mm per turn, tool centre #<ya> to #<yb>)
  #<vcnow> = [#<sout> * #<pi> * #<tdia> / 1000.0]
  (PRINT, ROTARY FACE: #<npass> passes, spindle #<sout> rpm held all cycle, surface speed #<vcnow> m per min)

  G90
  (BLEND THE CORNERS SO THE STOCK NEVER STOPS TURNING. The default here is)
  (G64 P0.001 -- a 1 micron tolerance, which cannot round anything -- so at)
  (the corner between the Y sweep and the Z dive the planner must arrive)
  (exactly on the corner point: Y decelerates to zero before Z starts, path)
  (velocity hits zero, and B stops with it. That is the pause between passes.)
  (P0.5 lets it miss the corner by half a millimetre and carry speed through)
  (an arc instead, so B keeps turning. Plain G64 is restored at the end.)
  G64 P0.5
  (SPINDLE FIRST, THEN TEN SECONDS, THEN MOVE. Operator 2026-08-12: "i want a)
  (10 second dwell after spindle before any movement ... to let it get to)
  (speed". The Mollom ramps a heavy tool holder over several seconds and)
  (there is no at-speed feedback wired, so the dwell IS the at-speed check.)
  (M3 also sits AHEAD of every G0 now: it used to be the last thing before)
  (the cutting loop, so anything that went wrong during the opening rapids)
  (took the spindle command down with it and the cycle fed a dead cutter.)
  M3 S#<sout>
  G4 P10
  (Z IS NOT TOUCHED ON THE WAY IN. The one and only Z rule in this cycle is)
  (the o18 gate above -- Z must already be above the stock radius -- and)
  (parking it is the operator's job, not this file's. Every Z command here)
  (after that point is a G1 dive of exactly the radial depth.)
  (Y CLEARS THE CHUCK BEFORE Z COMES DOWN. Operator 2026-08-12: "it needs to)
  (go so that the Y offset is respected BEFORE plunging. if i use a wide)
  (tool. it can plunge into the chuck if it plunges before moving in Y+".)
  (Y0 is the END of the stock, and the tool is a disc: standing there, half)
  (of it overhangs into the chuck. ya is one tool RADIUS in, which is the)
  (first Y at which the whole cutter is over material -- so the order is X,)
  (then Y, and only then Z.)
  G0 X0 Y[#<ya>]
  (RAPID DOWN TO D START. Z = rstart is the crown of the promised stock, so)
  (this is the last height that is still clear of it and the height every)
  (pass dives from. The o18 gate above has already refused anything lower.)
  G0 Z[#<rstart>]
  (NO G0 B0. B has no soft limits and winds on forever, so rapiding it back)
  (to zero first meant several full turns of the stock with Y parked at ya)
  (and nothing cut -- operator 2026-08-12: "its made like 4 or 5 rotations)
  (and Y is still stuck at 4.76". A facing cut only needs a starting angle,)
  (and wherever B is sitting is as good as any.)

  (ZIG-ZAG, NO RETURN TRIP. Operator 2026-08-12: dive, spiral 0 to Yend,)
  (dive, spiral back. The tool stays down and stays engaged; lifting and)
  (rapiding back to Y0 every pass would spend most of the cycle in air.)
  (B NEVER REVERSES -- only Y does. The worm is 1:20 and self-locking, so a)
  (direction change there is backlash for nothing, and B is a continuous)
  (rotary with no soft limits, so it can wind on forever.)
  (THE FIRST PASS CUTS AT D START, IT DOES NOT DIVE FIRST. Operator)
  (2026-08-12: "it should START at 70 finish a pass, then, go to 65". The)
  (loop used to subtract the radial depth before the first sweep, so the)
  (opening cut was already most of the way to the finished size and the)
  (promised diameter was never skimmed.)
  #<r>     = #<rstart>
  #<rprev> = #<rstart>
  #<pass>  = 0
  #<more>  = 1
  #<ycur> = #<ya>
  #<ytgt> = #<yb>
  #<dir>  = 1
  o100 while [#<more> GT 0]
    #<pass> = [#<pass> + 1]
    #<dnow> = [#<r> * 2.0]

    (SOLVE B AT THE UNCUT RADIUS -- the radius this pass is cutting FROM.)
    (On the first pass that is D START itself.)
    #<wb> = [3.0 * #<rf_chip> * #<sp> * #<rf_flutes> / [#<pi> * #<rprev>]]
    o30 if [#<wb> GT #<rf_bmax>]
      #<wb> = #<rf_bmax>
    o30 endif
    #<feed> = [#<pitch> * #<wb> / 6.0]
    #<brpm> = [#<wb> / 6.0]
    #<fzreal> = [#<wb> * #<pi> * #<rprev> / [3.0 * #<sout> * #<rf_flutes>]]

    (PRINT, ROTARY FACE: pass #<pass> at diameter #<dnow> - Z #<r>, B #<brpm> rpm, F #<feed>, chip load #<fzreal>)
    S#<sout>
    (THE DIVE TURNS THE STOCK TOO, so B never stops. Operator 2026-08-12:)
    ("B STOP at the end of a pass. it should never stop. just keep spining)
    (and only adjust speed up as you go to the next pass". A Z-only block)
    (parks B for the whole plunge; giving the block a full revolution of B)
    (makes it a helical entry at the same B speed the sweep just used, so)
    (the stock turns continuously from the first pass to the last.)
    (ONE REVOLUTION IS THE RIGHT AMOUNT: the tool is already engaged over)
    (the whole cut width, so the plunge spreads across a full turn instead)
    (of digging in at one angular position.)
    (RAMP IN, DO NOT PLUNGE. A separate Z-only dive is a 90 degree corner)
    (in the path: the planner has to swing from pure Y to pure Z, and even)
    (with G64 P0.5 there is only half a millimetre of arc to do it in, so B)
    (crawled under 5 deg/s for about 0.8 s at every corner -- measured)
    (2026-08-12, 4 samples under 5 and 19 under 40 out of 1336.)
    (Descending DURING the sweep removes the corner entirely: Y, Z and B all)
    (move together and nothing ever changes direction mid-pass. The only)
    (slowdown left is the Y reversal at the ends, which the operator accepts.)
    (ONE REVOLUTION OF STOCK PER RAMP. Y advances one pitch while Z drops the)
    (full radial depth and B turns once, so the entry is a helix at the same)
    (feed as the cut. Z rate works out at doc/pitch times the feed, which for)
    (a half-diameter stepover is exactly the feed -- well under the plunge)
    (ceiling below.)
    #<pmax>  = [#<tdia> * 18.9]
    #<pfeed> = #<feed>
    o106 if [#<pfeed> GT #<pmax>]
      #<pfeed> = #<pmax>
    o106 endif
    #<dz>   = [#<r> - #<rprev>]
    #<yrun> = #<len>
    #<brun> = [#<revs> * 360.0]
    o105 if [#<dz> LT 0]
      (SPINDLE UP THROUGH THE RAMP so the chip is lighter where the tool is)
      (engaging axially as well as radially -- operator 2026-08-12: "spin up)
      (spindle speed during the ramp to cut less". Same feed, twice the)
      (teeth per minute, so half the chip load. Capped at the drive ceiling.)
      #<sramp> = [#<sout> * 2.0]
      o107 if [#<sramp> GT #<rf_smax>]
        #<sramp> = #<rf_smax>
      o107 endif
      S#<sramp>
      (THE RAMP IS 6 x THE DEPTH OF Y, not one pitch. One pitch descended the)
      (whole radial depth in a single turn of the stock -- a plunge in all)
      (but name, and exactly what makes a fly cutter rub.)
      #<rrevs> = [#<ramp> / #<pitch>]
      G91
      G1 Y[#<ramp> * #<dir>] Z[#<dz>] B[#<rrevs> * 360.0] F[#<pfeed>]
      G90
      #<yrun> = [#<len> - #<ramp>]
      #<brun> = [#<brun> - [#<rrevs> * 360.0]]
    o105 endif

    (THE REST OF THE PASS AT DEPTH. One block, no corners: Y against B is a)
    (straight line, so a single G1 with both words IS the helix exactly.)
    S#<sout>
    G91
    G1 Y[#<yrun> * #<dir>] B[#<brun>] F[#<feed>]
    G90
    (SWAP THE ENDS: this pass finished where the next one starts.)
    #<ycur> = #<ytgt>
    o102 if [#<dir> GT 0]
      #<ytgt> = #<ya>
      #<dir>  = -1
    o102 else
      #<ytgt> = #<yb>
      #<dir>  = 1
    o102 endif
    (STEP DOWN, OR STOP IF THIS PASS WAS ALREADY THE FINAL DIAMETER.)
    o103 if [#<r> LE #<rend>]
      #<more> = 0
    o103 else
      #<rprev> = #<r>
      #<r> = [#<r> - #<doc>]
      o104 if [#<r> LT #<rend>]
        #<r> = #<rend>
      o104 endif
    o103 endif
  o100 endwhile

  G0 Y[#<ya>]
  G0 G53 Z0
  M5
  G64
  (PRINT, ROTARY FACE DONE: #<pass> passes, stock now #<rf_dend> mm over #<rf_len> of length.)
o<rotary_face> endsub
M2 (end program)
